// `rt`: the runtime compiled programs link, in Grasm (docs/self-hosting.md
// §6.7, stage S5), against today's ABI -- the C entry points of
// `libgrasm_rt_abi.a` and the memory layouts its values have.
//
// It moves one family of functions at a time, and until the last one has
// moved the Rust archive is linked behind it:
//
//     grasm tir compiler/rt/src/main.gr > rt.tir
//     s3 rt.tir -o rt.o --runtime
//     grasm-link <program objects> -interpose rt.o libgrasm_rt_abi.a ...
//
// Each `export fn grt_<name>` here is the program's `grasm_<name>`: the
// linker binds the program's references to it (`-interpose`), and the Grasm
// runtime's own `extern fn grasm_<name>` declarations still reach the
// archive's. Those are of two kinds, and `scripts/rtswap.sh` holds both to a
// list: a family that has not moved yet, and the *state* the Rust code that
// is still linked keeps using -- the box allocator and its live count, the
// output buffers, the panic and exit path -- which moves last, with the last
// of that code.
//
// The rules the code keeps:
//
//   * no Grasm heap value -- no `str`, list, map, struct or closure: only
//     the layout types and raw memory, so nothing here calls the runtime
//     it is part of to make one;
//   * no thread-local cell and no process-wide state: the program's cells
//     share one block with the runtime's, laid out by the program's own
//     compilation, and Grasm has no global;
//   * fixed text is code (`text.gr`, written by `gen_text.py`);
//   * every layout it reads or writes is in `layout.gr` and checked against
//     the Rust runtime's by `scripts/rtlayout.sh`.
//
// The families, each in its own module:
//
//     math     integer power, `%` on floats, abs/min/max, rounding, libm
//     panic    the panics' messages; the way out is still Rust's
//     fmt      integers and floats as text, bit-identical to Rust's `{}`
//     out      `print`'s pieces, written through the Rust runtime's buffer
//     numstr   numbers as string values and as interpolated pieces
//     strread  strings read: length in bytes, emptiness, equality, order
//     val      reference counting
//
// and three that serve them: `layout`, `text` and `sys` (the C library).
import math
import panic
import text
import layout
import fmt
import out
import numstr
import strread
import val
import sys
